    "colorFormatInfo": {
      "hex": "HEX writes a color as a six-digit code. It starts with # and combines 0–9 and a–f, like #ff0000 (red). It is the most common format on the web and in design tools, so it is handy for sharing or searching for a color.",
      "rgb": "RGB mixes the three primary colors of light (red, green, and blue), each as a number from 0 to 255. It matches how monitor pixels actually emit light, so it is the base model of computer graphics, but the numbers alone make it hard to picture the result.",
      "hsl": "HSL describes a color with hue (H), saturation (S), and lightness (L), close to how people perceive color. You adjust it in an intuitive order (which color, how vivid, how light), so it is easier to fine-tune than RGB.",
      "hsv": "HSV (or HSB) also uses hue (H), saturation (S), and value or brightness (V/B) like HSL, but 100% brightness means the most vivid color. It feels familiar because the color pickers in design tools such as Photoshop and Figma use it by default.",
      "oklch": "OKLCH is a color space recently added to web standards. It is designed so that lightness steps look even to the human eye, so different hues with the same L value look similarly bright. That makes it good for building colors of the same tone or palettes for dark and light themes."
    },
